      "message": "We use this pattern in a few places (combine header and payload). Let\u0027s create a helper to simplify and DRY the code. Here\u0027s Gemini-generated code. Check for correctness:\n\n----\n\n\n### 1\\. The Utility Function\n\nAdd this to `util/egis/egis_util.h`. If your C++23 standard library fully supports it, you can use `buffer.append_range()`. If not, standard `.insert()` works perfectly and is equally safe.\n\n```c\ntemplate \u003cTriviallyCopyable T\u003e\nstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e PackMessage(const T\u0026 header, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e payload \u003d {}) {\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e buffer;\n  // Pre-allocate the exact size to prevent reallocations\n  buffer.reserve(sizeof(T) + payload.size());\n  \n  auto header_bytes \u003d AsBytes(header);\n  \n  // Using standard insert \n  // Prefer C++23 buffer.append_range if it compiles.\n  buffer.insert(buffer.end(), header_bytes.begin(), header_bytes.end());\n  buffer.insert(buffer.end(), payload.begin(), payload.end());\n  \n  return buffer;\n}\n\n```\n\n### 2\\. The Implementation\n\nNow, you can replace those 6 lines of boilerplate in `WriteFlash` (and similarly in `SendBootromCommandSha`) with a single, highly readable function call.\n\n**In `WriteFlash`:**\n\n```c\n  EgisBootrom::BlockWriteCommand req_hdr{\n      .address \u003d ToLittleEndian(addr),\n      .count \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(data.size()))};\n\n  // One line. No manual offsets, no memcpy, no redundant error checking.\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e cmd \u003d PackMessage(req_hdr, data);\n\n  auto resp \u003d SendBootromCommand(cmd, kFlashTimeout);\n\n```\n\n**In `SendBootromCommandSha`:**\n\n```c\n  SecureCommandShaHeader req_hdr{\n      .algo \u003d std::to_underlying(wrapped_algo_),\n      .payload_len \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(data.size()))};\n  \n  crypto::Sha256(data, req_hdr.hash);\n\n  // Instantly pack the header and the payload\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e cmd \u003d PackMessage(req_hdr, data);\n\n  auto ret_res \u003d DoUsbTransfer(cmd, sizeof(SecureResponseHeader), timeout);\n\n```\n\n### Why this is better:\n\n1. **Zero `memcpy`:** You eliminate manual pointer arithmetic entirely.  \n2. **No Unreachable Error Handling:** In your original code, `WriteStruct` could technically return an error, forcing you to handle `kBufferTooSmall` even though you *just* created the vector to be the exact correct size. `PackMessage` guarantees sizing.  \n3. **DRY:** You reuse this anywhere you need to stick a header onto a payload.\n\n----\n\nHere is how the refactored `EcCommand` class in `util/egis/ec_comm.cc` looks when utilizing the new utility.\n\n### The Refactored `EcCommand`\n\nBy using `PackMessage`, we can completely delete the `PackCommand` helper method and handle everything safely in the constructor.\n\n**Current (`ec_comm.cc`):**\n\n```c\nclass EcCommand {\n public:\n  EcCommand(uint16_t cmd_id, uint8_t cmd_ver,\n            std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e payload \u003d {})\n      : cmd_id_(cmd_id), cmd_ver_(cmd_ver) {\n    PackCommand(payload);\n  }\n\n  const std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e \u0026bytes() const { return cmd_bytes_; }\n\n private:\n  uint16_t cmd_id_;\n  uint8_t cmd_ver_;\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e cmd_bytes_;\n\n  void PackCommand(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e payload) {\n    EcHostCommandRequest hdr \u003d {\n        .command_id \u003d ToLittleEndian(cmd_id_),\n        .command_version \u003d cmd_ver_,\n        .data_length \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint16_t\u003e(payload.size()))};\n    cmd_bytes_.resize(sizeof(hdr) + payload.size());\n    WriteStruct(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e(cmd_bytes_), hdr);\n\n    if (!payload.empty()) {\n      std::memcpy(cmd_bytes_.data() + sizeof(hdr), payload.data(),\n                  payload.size());\n    }\n    UpdateChecksum();\n  }\n\n  void UpdateChecksum() {\n    uint32_t sum \u003d 0;\n    // hdr.checksum is initialized to 0 by WriteStruct, so we can directly sum\n    for (uint8_t byte : cmd_bytes_) sum +\u003d byte;\n    cmd_bytes_[offsetof(EcHostCommandRequest, checksum)] \u003d\n        static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(-sum);\n  }\n};\n\n```\n\n**New \u0026 Improved (`ec_comm.cc`):**\n\n```c\nclass EcCommand {\n public:\n  EcCommand(uint16_t cmd_id, uint8_t cmd_ver, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e payload \u003d {}) {\n    \n    EcHostCommandRequest hdr \u003d {\n        .command_id \u003d ToLittleEndian(cmd_id),\n        .command_version \u003d cmd_ver,\n        .data_length \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint16_t\u003e(payload.size()))};\n\n    // 1. Pack the header and payload instantly without memcpy\n    cmd_bytes_ \u003d PackMessage(hdr, payload);\n    \n    // 2. Apply the checksum\n    UpdateChecksum();\n  }\n\n  const std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e \u0026bytes() const { return cmd_bytes_; }\n\n private:\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e cmd_bytes_;\n\n  void UpdateChecksum() {\n    uint32_t sum \u003d 0;\n    for (uint8_t byte : cmd_bytes_) sum +\u003d byte;\n    \n    // Safely map the header back over the vector rather than using offsetof\n    auto* hdr \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cEcHostCommandRequest*\u003e(cmd_bytes_.data());\n    hdr-\u003echecksum \u003d static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(-sum);\n  }\n};\n\n```\n\n### Why this is a massive improvement:\n\n1. **Deleted State:** We removed `cmd_id_` and `cmd_ver_` from the class entirely. They were only being stored temporarily to be used inside `PackCommand()`. Now, they are consumed directly in the constructor.  \n2. **Deleted Complexity:** The 12 lines of memory resizing, struct writing, and empty-payload checking are replaced by one simple `PackMessage` call.  \n3. **Fixed Security Issue:** The `UpdateChecksum` method now uses `reinterpret_cast` to access the `checksum` field. If someone modifies `EcHostCommandRequest` to include padding in the future, `offsetof` would have silently written to the wrong memory address. Reinterpreting the struct guarantees memory safety.\n\n----\n\nWe can also use it in `SendBootromCommandAes`\n\nSince we are trading the zero-copy approach for safety, we can now use the `PackMessage` utility here as well. The workflow becomes remarkably straightforward: generate the ciphertext into a temporary buffer, and then pack the header and ciphertext together.\n\nHere is the fully refactored `SendBootromCommandAes` incorporating the hoisted payload check, compile-time spans, visual chunking, and the new `PackMessage` utility.\n\n### `util/egis/bootrom.cc`\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e, BootromError\u003e\nBootromComm::SendBootromCommandAes(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data,\n                                   std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) {\n  \n  SecureCommandAesHeader req_hdr{\n      .algo \u003d std::to_underlying(wrapped_algo_),\n      .payload_len \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(data.size()))};\n\n  uint32_t aad_val \u003d ToLittleEndian(EgisBootrom::kChipIdAad);\n\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------\n  // PHASE 1: Encrypt\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e ciphertext(data.size());\n  {\n    if (auto res \u003d crypto::GenerateGcmIv(req_hdr.iv); !res) {\n      std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] IV Generation Failed\");\n      return std::unexpected(BootromError::kGcmIvGenerationFailed);\n    }\n\n    if (auto res \u003d crypto::AesGcmEncrypt(aes_key_, req_hdr.iv, AsBytes(aad_val), \n                                         data, ciphertext, req_hdr.tag); !res) {\n      std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] Encryption Failed\");\n      return std::unexpected(BootromError::kGcmEncryptionFailed);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------\n  // PHASE 2: Pack and Transmit\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------\n  std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e secure_data; \n  auto ret_res \u003d DoUsbTransfer(PackMessage(req_hdr, ciphertext), \n                               BootromComm::kScHeaderSizeAes, timeout);\n  {\n    if (!ret_res) return std::unexpected(ret_res.error());\n\n    auto extracted \u003d ExtractSecureData(ret_res.value(), BootromComm::kScHeaderSizeAes);\n    if (!extracted) return std::unexpected(extracted.error());\n    \n    secure_data \u003d extracted.value();\n  }\n\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------\n  // PHASE 3: Unpack and Decrypt Response\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e decrypted_payload;\n  {\n    auto rsp_iv \u003d secure_data.first\u003ccrypto::kAesGcmIvSize\u003e();\n    auto rsp_tag \u003d secure_data.subspan\u003ccrypto::kAesGcmIvSize, crypto::kAesGcmTagSize\u003e();\n    auto rsp_ciphertext \u003d secure_data.subspan(crypto::kAesGcmIvSize + crypto::kAesGcmTagSize);\n\n    decrypted_payload.resize(rsp_ciphertext.size());\n\n    if (auto res \u003d crypto::AesGcmDecrypt(aes_key_, rsp_iv, AsBytes(aad_val), \n                                         rsp_ciphertext, rsp_tag, decrypted_payload); !res) {\n      std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] Decryption Failed\");\n      return std::unexpected(BootromError::kGcmAuthenticationFailed);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return decrypted_payload;\n}\n\n```",
