      "side": 1,
      "message": "We can simplify this a bit with `string_view` and `std::ranges::find`.\n\nGemini-generated code. Check that it\u0027s correct:\n\n----\n\n```cpp\nstd::println(\"  Product Name: {}\", std::string_view(info.prod_name, std::ranges::find(info.prod_name, \u0027\\0\u0027)));\n\n```\n\n### Why this is the ultimate approach here:\n\n1. **Zero State:** You don\u0027t leave a `prod_name` variable hanging around in the local scope that you don\u0027t need anymore.\n2. **Safety First:** You might wonder, *\"Why not just pass `info.prod_name` directly to `std::println`?\"* If you do that (`std::println(\"{}\", info.prod_name)`), the formatter will treat it as a standard C-string. If the product name happens to take up exactly 8 bytes and lacks a null terminator, the formatter will read past the end of the array into unallocated memory, causing Undefined Behavior (UB).\n3. **Clean Output:** By using `ranges::find` as the boundary, you ensure that if the name is shorter than 8 bytes, `std::println` won\u0027t print out the trailing `\\0` characters as empty spaces or garbage.\n\nThis inline `std::string_view` guarantees memory safety, handles the null-trimming perfectly, and keeps your source code extremely concise.",
