      "writtenOn": "2026-05-28T18:53:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In `crypto_util.h`, you accept dynamically sized spans and check their sizes at runtime inside the implementation. C++20 allows you to enforce these sizes at **compile-time** using fixed-extent spans. You actually did this perfectly in `BootromComm::SetAesKey`, but missed it in the core crypto functions.\n\n**Current (`crypto_util.h`):**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmEncrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e key, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e iv, ...);\n\n```\n\n**Better:** Enforce the exact byte lengths in the signature. If a caller tries to pass a dynamically sized vector without explicitly converting it, or an array of the wrong size, the compiler will catch it.\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmEncrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAes256KeySize\u003e key,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAesGcmIvSize\u003e iv,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e aad, \n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e plaintext,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e ciphertext, \n    std::span\u003cuint8_t, kAesGcmTagSize\u003e tag);\n\n```\n\n*(You can then remove the runtime `key.size() !\u003d kAes256KeySize` checks from `crypto_util.cc` entirely).*",
