      "message": "When you receive the `GetVersionResponse` from the EC, you manually force a null terminator at the very end of the arrays to prevent buffer overruns before printing them.\n\n**Current (`et171_flash.cc`):**\n\n```c\n  // Manually mutating the struct to force null-termination\n  response.ro_ver[sizeof(response.ro_ver) - 1] \u003d \u0027\\0\u0027;\n  response.rw_ver[sizeof(response.rw_ver) - 1] \u003d \u0027\\0\u0027;\n  \n  std::println(\"\\n--- Parsed Version Info ---\");\n  std::println(\"RO Version: {}\", response.ro_ver);\n  std::println(\"RW Version: {}\", response.rw_ver);\n\n```\n\n**The Idiomatic C++23 Way:** You can completely delete the manual null-termination lines. Because you shouldn\u0027t mutate parsed network data just to print it, you can use the exact same inline `std::string_view` \\+ `std::ranges::find` pattern.\n\n```c\n  std::println(\"\\n--- Parsed Version Info ---\");\n  std::println(\"RO Version: {}\", std::string_view(response.ro_ver, std::ranges::find(response.ro_ver, \u0027\\0\u0027)));\n  std::println(\"RW Version: {}\", std::string_view(response.rw_ver, std::ranges::find(response.rw_ver, \u0027\\0\u0027)));\n\n```\n\n*(Note: The `GetVersionResponse` also has `fwid_ro` and `fwid_rw` fields, which you could safely print using this exact same one-liner if you decide to expose them later).*",
